A||N||5||Monkeyman||Sensor questions||||5||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||02-02-2001||10:42 PM||||My GT just got new plug wires, rotor and cap (neat little light show in the dark if I sprayed some water on the engine).  I get 5 codes when the car is hooked to a OBDII monitor.  Here they are:<BR>**13--O2 sensor  Absolutely need this one since I can see where the sensor is missing.  (See other thread regarding this.)<BR>**21--TPS sensor (high voltage)  <BR>**24--VSS no signal  The speedo works fine, but I don't have any cruise control.  Maybe this is the root of the problem?<BR>**25--MAT sensor (intake air temp high)<BR>**32--EGR valve  (I'm guessing/hoping it's the selenoid.)<P>Since the Grape Ape wasn't throwing any codes and isn't being driven currently, I plan on swapping the TPS & MAT sensors and EGR selenoid with them.  I'll definitely need a new O2 sensor (see other thread).  The shop suggested that the VSS-no signal could possibly be a wiring problem (makes sense since the check engine light has been on forever).  They also suggested that one of these codes/problems could be the cause of some of the rest.<P>Any thoughts on any of this?||||reg||5
Z||000001||mrfiero||02-02-2001||11:57 PM||||LMK what you find out on the code 24......my silver GT sets this code too and my cruise doesn't work either.  I replaced the VSS with a known good one (from my 16K mile GT) and it still sets this code.  I also replaced the cruise module and turn stalk with no luck either. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Monkeyman||02-03-2001||12:05 AM||||Mike--Where is the VSS (guessing it's a sensor of some sort?)?  CC worked fine in the Grape Ape so I might swap VSSs too, just to be sure about this whole thing.  The more I do, the less time it should take them to check voltages, etc.  At $43/hr, I'd like to eliminate as many problems as I can before the mechanic gets his greasy little hands on it.||||reg||1
Z||000003||mrfiero||02-03-2001||12:40 AM||||The VSS is located below the alternator on the tranny (of course).  There is an electrical plug and a retaining clip.  Unplug it and pull the retaining clip straight up. The VSS just slides out (towards the passenger side).  <P>I have no problems removing it from my '88 GT, but I think you may have some interference on the early suspension (not sure).<P>There is a groove in the case of the VSS......make sure it is pointing towards the front of the car when you reinstall it......the retaining clip slides into this groove to hold it in place.<P>LMK what you find out.  I can't pass the emissions test here.....if the SES light comes on it's an automatic fail.||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||02-03-2001||11:56 AM||||If the speedo works then it is a problem with the dash, the wiring, or the ECM, NOT the VSS. The VSS feed goes to the dash first.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||02-04-2001||02:34 AM||||Can't be a problem with the dash since I've swapped out to one from an '87 GT.  WTIII (the previous owner) said that he swapped ECMs with a known good one (temporarily) so that's probably not the problem either.  I hate wiring problems since they tend to get expensive to diagnose, but...<P>I plan on swapping out some of the sensors tomorrow (today?).  MAT, EGR selenoid, and TPS from the Grape Ape (no codes) to the GT.  I'll reset the ECM and see what codes it throws then.  I found a good deal on a used crossover pipe, so I'll have the mechanics change that and install a new O2 sensor sometime towards the end of the week.  Once I lose all of the codes (except VSS, I guess), I can have the mechanics spend some time finding the wiring problem.  Maybe I'll gain CC once that's fixed.||||reg||1
